# punused findings adjudicated as false positives. One literal substring per
# line, matched against punused's output line; full-line `#` comments carry the
# reason.
#
# `action` is a named string type carrying an explicit `var _ fmt.Stringer`
# assertion in config.go, and cfg.Action is passed as a slog attr value from
# main.go, daemon.go and scheduler.go. Production uses slogx.Setup's text
# handler, and a named string type misses slog's concrete-string fast path, so
# the KindAny path formats it with fmt and dispatches to String(). Its two
# siblings runMode.String and phaseOutcome.String are not flagged only because
# they also have direct callers. Upstream: bep/punused#3.
method (action).String

#
# `(*capbuf.Buffer).Write` is called only through io.Writer dispatch inside the
# stdlib: scheduler.go hands the buffer to io.MultiWriter and exec.Cmd.Stderr,
# and those call Write from stdlib code gopls does not index as a reference.
# Before the ioutil split the same package happened to contain linefilter's own
# `fw.w.Write` interface call site, which masked the blind spot; the split
# moved the only in-workspace io.Writer.Write call into another package and
# surfaced it. Same class as bep/punused#3 above: interface/dynamic dispatch
# invisible to the reference scan.
method (*Buffer).Write
